# One paragraph per section, subsection and appendix, each sentence grounded in the 1-3 body paragraphs that
# establish it. Status ai-draft. The acknowledgements have no brief.
BRIEF = {
    "sec-intro": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-intro-p1 sec-intro-p2">Type II strings on $AdS_3\times S^3\times T^4$ are dual to a 2d SCFT with a free symmetric orbifold limit; the paper highlights three confusing features of their string perturbation theory.</span> <span data-covers="sec-intro-p4">The main result: the free orbifold's grand-canonical correlators, unlike fixed-$N$ ones, have an exact string genus expansion.</span> <span data-covers="sec-intro-p5">Separately, the orbifold's $1/N$ expansion breaks down where the NS-NS string is strongly coupled, and the S-dual R-R string is never weakly coupled.</span> <span data-covers="sec-intro-p6">And an extra $\hat T^4$ is decoupled from strings, allowed because in the bulk it is a topological Chern–Simons theory.</span></p>""",

    "sec-review": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-sugra-p2 sec-sugra-p3">Supergravity gives two S-dual frames: one with R-R flux, and one with purely NS-NS flux, where the 6d coupling is fixed, $g_6'^2=Q_5/Q_1$, and the torus volume is free.</span> <span data-covers="sec-dual_cft-p1 sec-dual_cft-p4">The dual CFT is a sigma model on an instanton moduli space; for one unit of NS5 flux it becomes $(T^4)^N/S_N\times\hat T^4$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-string_ws-p4">For $Q_5=1$ the NS-NS worldsheet reproduces this free orbifold's spectrum and correlators, strongly suggesting the two are dual.</span> <span data-covers="sec-diffrent_back-p1 sec-diffrent_back-p2">All coprime $(Q_1,Q_5)$ with the same $N$ are U-dual, so their CFTs are exactly marginal deformations of that orbifold.</span> <span data-covers="section-2.5-p1">Three separate questions follow: an ensemble over $Q_1$, a free orbifold at strong coupling, and a decoupled $\hat T^4$.</span></p>""",

    "sec-sugra": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-sugra-p1">Near the horizon, five of the 25 massless scalars of string theory on $T^4$ are fixed and 20 remain moduli.</span> <span data-covers="sec-sugra-p2">In the R-R frame the AdS radius and torus volume follow from $Q_1$, $Q_5$ and a free coupling, with $R^2/\alpha'=g_{10}Q_5$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-sugra-p3">S-duality to the NS-NS frame fixes the 6d coupling instead, $g_6'^2=Q_5/Q_1$, so the torus volume $v_4$ becomes free and $g_{10}'^2=v_4Q_5/Q_1$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-sugra-p2 sec-sugra-p3">Newton's constants in AdS units agree in both frames, with $G_N^{(10)}/R^8=v_4/(Q_1Q_5^3)$.</span></p>""",

    "sec-dual_cft": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-dual_cft-p1">The dual SCFT is a sigma model on the moduli space of $Q_1$ instantons of $U(Q_5)$ on $T^4$, with central charge $6Q_1Q_5+6$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-dual_cft-p3">For $Q_5>1$, zero-size instantons open throats with a continuous spectrum.</span> <span data-covers="sec-dual_cft-p4">For $Q_5=1$ the target becomes $(T^4)^N/S_N$ times a torus $\hat T^4$ from $U(1)$ Wilson lines.</span> <span data-covers="sec-dual_cft-p4">A theta angle at the orbifold singularity must be fixed; the paper reviews arguments for $\theta=\pi$, the free orbifold.</span> <span data-covers="sec-dual_cft-p5">The SCFT's $8+8$ $U(1)$ currents have levels $Q_1$ and $Q_5$, matching bulk NS-NS and R-R gauge fields.</span></p>""",

    "sec-string_ws": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-string_ws-p1">Strings with R-R flux cannot yet be quantised, unlike those with NS-NS flux.</span> <span data-covers="sec-string_ws-p2">With NS-NS flux the RNS worldsheet is a sum of WZW models at level $Q_5$ and a $T^4$ sigma model, valid for $Q_5\ge2$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-string_ws-p3">It has a continuum of long strings, matching the CFT's small-instanton continuum.</span> <span data-covers="sec-string_ws-p4">The hybrid formalism works for any $Q_5$; at $Q_5=1$ it has no continuum and reproduces the symmetric orbifold's correlators. With the CFT arguments, this strongly suggests the $(N,1)$ NS-NS background is dual to the free orbifold with $\theta=\pi$.</span></p>""",

    "sec-diffrent_back": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-diffrent_back-p1">U-duality relates all backgrounds with coprime $(Q_1,Q_5)$ and the same $N$, but maps weak coupling in one to strong coupling in the others.</span> <span data-covers="sec-diffrent_back-p2">Turning on R-R scalars is exactly marginal, so all these CFTs are deformations of the free orbifold.</span> <span data-covers="sec-diffrent_back-p2">Each $(Q_1,Q_5)$ is reached by blowing up the orbifold singularity and by $J\bar J$ deformations mixing $T^4$ and $\hat T^4$ currents.</span> <span data-covers="sec-diffrent_back-p3">The 64 $J\bar J$ deformations are boundary conditions of the bulk Chern–Simons fields.</span> <span data-covers="fig-N30_moduli sec-diffrent_back-p5">A slice for $N=30$ shows where each background sits, and where the $\theta=0$ orbifold is believed to lie.</span></p>""",

    "section-2.5": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-2.5-p1">The review raises three questions. How can NS-NS strings, which describe an ensemble over $Q_1$, match a fixed-$N$ orbifold? How is the orbifold free at any torus volume, although strings become strongly coupled once $v_4>N$?</span> <span data-covers="section-2.5-p1">And how is the CFT's decoupled $\hat T^4$, absent from the string-orbifold matching, realised in strings?</span> <span data-covers="section-2.5-p2">Sections 3, 4 and 5 answer them in turn, and the paper says they are almost completely independent of each other.</span></p>""",

    "sec-st_grand_can": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-st_grand_can-p1 sec-st_grand_can-p2">Perturbative strings on the NS-NS background compute an ensemble of CFTs summed over $Q_1$, not a single CFT.</span> <span data-covers="section-3.1-p3 section-3.1-p4">This is because $Q_1$ never appears on the worldsheet; its place in the central charge is taken by an operator $I$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-genus_exp-p7 sec-genus_exp-p8">For $Q_5=1$ the free orbifold at fixed $N$ matches string perturbation theory only at leading order in $1/N$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-grand-p4 sec-grand-p6">Summed over $N$ with weight $p^N$, its correlators exponentiate into connected coverings and form an exact genus expansion with $g_s^{-2}\propto p$.</span> <span data-covers="section-3.5-p1 section-3.5-p3">For $Q_5>1$ a Laplace transform over $Q_1$ is conjectured, and for $Q_5=1$ perturbation theory appears complete, without non-perturbative corrections.</span></p>""",

    "section-3.1": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-3.1-p2">The near-horizon limit suggests a fixed $Q_1$ and a fixed 6d coupling.</span> <span data-covers="section-3.1-p3">Yet the NS-NS worldsheet action depends only on $Q_5$, with a free genus-counting coupling $g_s$ tied to no spacetime field.</span> <span data-covers="section-3.1-p4">The spacetime central charge comes out as $c=6Q_5I$, with $I$ a worldsheet operator that is not a constant, so no single CFT can be dual.</span> <span data-covers="section-3.1-p5">A coupling for $I$ is absorbed into $g_s$, so the string has one parameter, suggested to be the fugacity $p$ of an ensemble over $Q_1$ weighted by $p^{Q_1}$.</span></p>""",

    "sec-genus_exp": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-genus_exp-p2 sec-genus_exp-p3">Twist-operator correlators of the fixed-$N$ orbifold are sums over covering surfaces, identified with string worldsheets, and the paper normalises twist operators so that each permutation counts once.</span> <span data-covers="sec-genus_exp-p6">With unit-normalised operators a genus-$g$ covering scales as $N^{1-g-n/2}$, suggesting $g_s^2=1/N$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-genus_exp-p7">But this holds only at leading order, because $1/N$ corrections to planar diagrams mix with genus one, and no redefinition separates them.</span> <span data-covers="sec-genus_exp-p8">Disconnected pieces also differ from products of their parts by $1+O(1/N)$, because their cycles may not overlap, so the string's cluster decomposition fails.</span></p>""",

    "sec-grand": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-grand-p2">The grand-canonical partition function is $Z_p[J]=\sum_N p^N Z_N[J]$, summing partition functions, not normalised correlators.</span> <span data-covers="sec-grand-p3 sec-grand-p4">At fixed $N$ the coverings must use exactly $N$ copies; without that constraint the sum exponentiates into connected coverings weighted by $p^{N_c}/N_c$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-grand-p6">Rescaling each twist operator by $p^{-w/2}$ then gives every genus-$g$ diagram exactly the string weight, with $g_s^{-2}\propto p$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-grand-p7">String theory thus computes ratios of $Z_p$, and fixed-$N$ correlators need separate inverse Laplace transforms.</span> <span data-covers="sec-grand-p8 sec-grand-p10">The copy-counting operator should correspond to $I$, and its exchange spoils field-theory clustering.</span></p>""",

    "sec-higher_genus_CFT": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-higher_genus_CFT-p1">The ensemble formula holds on any surface, with coverings now also summing over $S_N$ holonomies.</span> <span data-covers="sec-higher_genus_CFT-p2 sec-higher_genus_CFT-p3">On a surface of genus $G>1$ the worldsheet must cover it, so its genus is at least $G$, and the coupling becomes $p\propto g_s^{2G-2}$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-higher_genus_CFT-p3">Large $p$ is then strong coupling, and tree-level worldsheets never contribute; the paper expects this not to persist for $Q_5>1$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-higher_genus_CFT-p4">On the torus the worldsheet genus is fixed by the insertions alone.</span></p>""",

    "section-3.5": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-3.5-p1">For $Q_5>1$ the natural guess is that the NS-NS string is a Laplace transform over $Q_1$, $Z_{p,Q_5}=\sum_{Q_1}p^{Q_5Q_1}Z_{Q_1,Q_5}$, refining the earlier Legendre transform, which approximates it at large $Q_1$. Whether $Q_1$ not coprime to $Q_5$ belong in the sum is left open.</span> <span data-covers="section-3.5-p2">Fixed-$Q_1$ correlators follow only with operators normalised by suitable powers of $p$, as the known insertions of $I$ suggest.</span> <span data-covers="section-3.5-p3">For $Q_5=1$ the sphere result is an exact equality, so perturbative strings seem to capture everything, a strong form of background independence.</span></p>""",

    "sec-breakdown": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-4.1-p1">The orbifold is free for any torus volume $V$, while the dual NS-NS string's squared 10d coupling is $g_{10}^2=V/N$.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.1-p2">The puzzle resolves because the orbifold's $1/N$ expansion goes in powers of $V/N$, so it breaks down where the string coupling grows.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.1-p4">The S-dual R-R string, apparently weakly coupled there, is not, because its 10d Newton constant in AdS units, $V/N$, is large.</span> <span data-covers="sec-planck-p3 sec-planck-p4">Counting coverings shows that the expansion also fails at high energies, $E\sim N^{1/6}$ or lower, a negative power of the string coupling.</span></p>""",

    "section-4.1": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-4.1-p1">In the NS-NS frame the squared 10d string coupling is $g_{10}^2=V/N$, so perturbation theory should fail once the torus volume reaches $N$.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.1-p2">The orbifold agrees: genus-$g$ terms of its $1/N$ expansion carry $(V/N)^g$, from torus zero modes on loops around branch points.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.1-p3">S-duality gives an R-R description with $g_{10}^2=N/V$, apparently weakly coupled for $V\gg N$.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.1-p4">But its AdS radius is then below the string scale, and Newton's constant in AdS units, $G_N^{(10)}/R^8=V/N$, is large. So for $Q_5=1$ the R-R string is never weakly coupled.</span></p>""",

    "sec-planck": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-planck-p2">Operators of high energy $E$ come mostly from twisted sectors of cycle length $w\sim E$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-planck-p3">Counting covering graphs, the number of genus-$g$ diagrams grows as $E^{3(2g-2)+2n}$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-planck-p4">If single diagrams do not grow with energy, the $1/N$ expansion fails at $E\sim N^{1/6}$; if they grow, possibly earlier.</span> <span data-covers="sec-planck-p4">Either way the failure sets in at a negative power of the string coupling.</span></p>""",

    "sec-decoupled_sec": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-q5_1_dec_sector-p5 sec-q5_1_dec_sector-p6">For $Q_5=1$ the CFT's extra $\hat T^4$ carries the R-R charges of wrapped D1- and D3-branes, and matching the bulk BPS bound gives it the inverse volume, $\hat G=G/V$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-q5_1_dec_sector-p8 sec-q5_1_dec_sector-p9">Its currents appear to have no local worldsheet vertex; in the bulk it is a level-1 Chern–Simons sector that decouples from all strings.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.2-p10 section-5.2-p11">Other $(Q_1,Q_5)$ need a blow-up of the orbifold plus a $J\bar J$ deformation, found exactly.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.3-p2 section-5.3-p3">For $Q_5>1$ the deformed currents have levels $Q_1$ and $Q_5$, and only the level-$Q_1$ ones have worldsheet vertex operators.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.3-p4 section-5.3-p6">States with charges that are multiples of these levels are pure gauge modes, not D-branes, though they carry R-R charge.</span></p>""",

    "sec-q5_1_dec_sector": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-q5_1_dec_sector-p2 sec-q5_1_dec_sector-p3">The CFT splits into a coset CFT and a Sugawara CFT of its 16 currents, whose energy bounds each state's energy.</span> <span data-covers="sec-q5_1_dec_sector-p5 sec-q5_1_dec_sector-p6">Matching it to the bulk BPS bound identifies the $\hat T^4$ charges with wrapped D1 and D3 charges and gives the inverse volume, $\hat G=G/V$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-q5_1_dec_sector-p8">Since the $\hat T^4$ partition function is an overall factor, the paper argues its currents have no local worldsheet vertex.</span> <span data-covers="sec-q5_1_dec_sector-p9">In the bulk this sector is a level-1 Chern–Simons theory, dual by itself to the $\hat T^4$ CFT, so D-branes charged under it should not exist.</span></p>""",

    "section-5.2": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-5.2-p1 section-5.2-p3">Backgrounds are labelled by a charge matrix and two moduli related by the attractor mechanism.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.2-p4">The moduli slice is a fundamental domain of $\Gamma_0(N)$, the U-duality subgroup fixing the $(N,1)$ background.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.2-p5 section-5.2-p6">Each coprime $(Q_1,Q_5)$ background with vanishing R-R scalars maps to an arc in this domain, joining its NS-NS and R-R weak-coupling ends.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.2-p10">Matching the bulk BPS bound fixes the $J\bar J$ deformation of the Sugawara CFT exactly: $M^{12}=M^{21}=-\frac{\chi}{N}G$ mixes the two tori's currents, and $\frac{\chi^2}{N}G$ shifts the $\hat T^4$ moduli.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.2-p11">The accompanying blow-up of the coset CFT is not followed.</span></p>""",

    "section-5.3": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-5.3-p2">Rewritten in $(Q_1,Q_5)$ charges, the deformed Sugawara energy splits into currents of levels $Q_1$ and $Q_5$, matching the bulk Chern–Simons theory.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.3-p3">Only the level-$Q_1$ NS-NS currents have worldsheet vertex operators, as found earlier for $Q_5>1$.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.3-p4">States saturating the bound, the BPS lattice, have string charges divisible by $Q_1$ and R-R charges divisible by $Q_5$, and decouple from the coset CFT.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.3-p5 section-5.3-p6">They carry R-R charge yet are pure bulk gauge modes, not D-branes, as for $C_3$ holonomies in a background with $H_3$ flux.</span></p>""",

    "app-orbifold": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-A.1-p2 section-A.1-p4">For compact bosons, the Sugawara energy of winding and momentum is derived, and $J\bar J$ deformations are shown to shift the moduli exactly.</span> <span data-covers="section-A.2-p3 section-A.2-p5">For the orbifold times $\hat T^4$, the explicit $J\bar J$ deformation of section 5 reproduces the $\chi$-dependent BPS bound.</span></p>""",

    "section-A.1": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-A.1-p1">A sigma model on $T^d$ with metric $G$ and $B$-field has momentum and winding currents.</span> <span data-covers="section-A.1-p2 section-A.1-p3">Their charges fix a Sugawara energy, a T-duality-invariant lower bound on $L_0$, given by the current two-point functions.</span> <span data-covers="section-A.1-p4">A $J\bar J$ deformation changes $E=G+B$ exactly, $\partial_\lambda E=EME$, and such deformations span the whole moduli space.</span></p>""",

    "section-A.2": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-A.2-p1 section-A.2-p2">For the orbifold, the currents summed over the $N$ copies have integer charges in every twisted sector.</span> <span data-covers="section-A.2-p3">Their Sugawara energy is that of one torus with the orbifold block divided by $N$.</span> <span data-covers="section-A.2-p5">The $J\bar J$ deformation of section 5, mixing orbifold and $\hat T^4$ currents, reproduces the $\chi$-dependent bulk bound exactly.</span></p>""",
}
